= Program (computing) =

== Overview ==
A '''computer program''' is an executable or interpretable specification of computational behavior. Programs are concrete artifacts through which people express instructions, definitions, control, and data manipulation for a computing system.

A program is related to an [[Algorithm|algorithm]], but the two are not identical. An algorithm is a method; a program expresses behavior in a particular [[Programming language|programming language]] or machine representation and usually contains much more than a single algorithm.

== Program as artifact ==
A program can exist before it is running. Source code, bytecode, machine code, stored procedures, scripts, and firmware images can all function as program representations in different environments.

When a program executes, the running instance is usually modeled as a '''process''' or runtime instance with current state, resources, inputs, and interactions. One stored program can therefore have many processes running at the same time.

== Program, computation, and software ==
These terms describe different levels.

* A [[Algorithm|algorithm]] is a method or procedure.
* A [[Computation|computation]] is the carrying out of computational state transitions.
* A '''program''' is an artifact/specification that directs computational behavior.
* [[Software]] is a broader engineered system or artifact set that may contain many programs, libraries, configuration, schemas, data, interfaces, and documentation.

The distinctions become especially important in operating systems, distributed systems, reproducibility, and security.

== Structure ==
Programs commonly include some combination of:

* instructions or expressions;
* [[Data structure|data structures]] and values;
* control flow;
* functions/procedures/modules;
* state;
* input/output;
* error handling;
* interfaces to files, networks, devices, databases, or other programs;
* concurrency or event handling.

Not every programming model exposes these concepts in the same way.

== Source, translation, and execution ==
A source program is written in a programming language or another executable notation. Depending on the implementation, it may then be:

* compiled ahead of time;
* translated to bytecode or another intermediate representation;
* interpreted;
* just-in-time compiled;
* linked with libraries;
* executed within an operating system, browser, virtual machine, database, device, or specialized runtime.

"Compiled" and "interpreted" are therefore often properties of an implementation strategy rather than permanent properties of a language.

== Program behavior and environment ==
A program's observed behavior depends on more than source text. Inputs, dependencies, configuration, environment variables, operating-system behavior, hardware, network services, timing, and other concurrent components may all matter.

This is why reproducing a program's behavior may require capturing its execution environment as well as its source.

== Correctness ==
A program is correct only relative to some specification or expected behavior.

Evidence can come from:

* testing;
* static analysis;
* type checking;
* model checking;
* formal verification;
* runtime monitoring;
* review.

No single technique proves every property of every program. Tests, for example, provide evidence for exercised cases but do not generally establish universal correctness.

== Programs that do not terminate ==
Many useful programs are intentionally long-running: operating-system services, network servers, interactive applications, controllers, and event loops.

For these programs, useful correctness properties include safety, liveness, responsiveness, protocol conformance, or continued service rather than eventual termination.

== Boundary cases ==
Scripts, notebooks, smart contracts, database procedures, build files, workflows, and declarative configuration can contain executable semantics. Whether a specific artifact is called a "program" can depend on the execution model and context.

When classification matters, describe what interprets the artifact and what behavior it can cause.

== Relationship to version control ==
Programs are commonly managed with [[Version control]], which records revisions and supports comparison, collaboration, branching, merging, and recovery. Version history records changes to a program; it does not by itself establish that any revision is correct.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Computer program" is established terminology, but software platforms blur boundaries among programs, configuration, data, models, and generated artifacts. BITwiki should state the execution model rather than treating every executable-looking file as the same kind of object.

